<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otumba</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Otumba de Gómez Farías</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otumba is a town in the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, close to the ruins of Teotihuacan. Nearby obsidian sources were worked by the Teotihuacanos and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, for whom Otumba was a craft centre. On the plain near the town <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> defeated an Aztec army in July 1520, during the Spanish retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otumba.jpg" alt="Otumba" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thelmadatter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PlazaOtumbaMX.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
